BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 304.92 Tm (Recovery timing is another choice the practitioners do not reduce to a universal schedule. Recurly reports a 47%) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 289.70 Tm (merchant median recovery rate for dunning windows of one to seven days and 57.7% for fifteen to twenty-one days.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 274.47 Tm (The speaker observes higher recovery with longer cycles, then recommends experiments because individual businesses) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 259.25 Tm (know their customers and differ from one another. Freemius describes its included retries as one day later, then three,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 244.02 Tm (then five. Churnkey's roundtable instead works through a month-long example: four Stripe retries, another four or five) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 228.80 Tm (precision attempts, and emails spread across the customer's grace period. For a 30-day grace period, the speaker gives) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 213.57 Tm (days 1, 5, 7, 15, and 28 as an example and places the final email one or two days before access ends. RonBuilds starts) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 198.35 Tm (from observed payback behavior: determine when recovery usually happens and put communications in that period,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 183.12 Tm (whether the first two weeks or the first month. He also limits deep personalization for customers who never activated,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 167.90 Tm (favoring a general dunning sequence while the team fixes onboarding. The alternative playbooks therefore differ both) Tj ET
